      "content": "SRC_003 asserts that applying a pass twice is equivalent to applying it once, but also allows a pass to introduce previously unseen warrants. That allowance appears to break idempotence: a first run could add warrant W1, promoting node A from UNDEC to IN; a second run on the expanded graph could add W2, demoting B from OUT to IN and consequently pulling A back to UNDEC. Thus pass-1 after pass-1 yields a different outcome than pass-1 alone. However, this objection fails against the target as written. The target never says a pass may generate an arbitrary, reapplication-dependent sequence of warrants; it only says a pass 'may commit new warrants,' which is compatible with a deterministic, state-derived fixpoint. The target's explicit 'fresh unique fixpoint' clause requires that a pass applied to the graph it produced must return that same fixpoint, ruling out the W2 scenario. Moreover, the target only permits status changes in one direction—from suspended to IN—and explicitly forbids relabeling existing statuses; the proposed regressive IN→UNDEC change contradicts that. The counterexample's assumptions of non-unique fixpoints and regressive flips are unsupported and even contradicted. Consequently, the idempotence conjecture, read together with the uniqueness clause, remains internally consistent: each pass computes a single fixpoint, and composition with itself reproduces it. The alleged fault is not established."
